    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A well-established sign of early-stage Alzheimer’s disease (AD) is degeneration of the noradrenergic network, the circuit of neurons charged with producing and distributing the neurotransmitter noradrenaline (NA) throughout the brain. Thus, a substantial decrease in NA signaling is believed to underlie AD-related cognitive deficits and pathological changes within the brain. Studies have demonstrated that reduction or inhibition of NA signaling exacerbates cognitive symptoms of AD in mice and humans, and reduction of NA signaling has also been shown to increase AD-related neuropathological changes in animal models. Collectively, such findings suggest that activation of the adrenergic system could alleviate AD progression. Previous mouse studies on the therapeutic potential of beta-adrenergic system activation have utilized compounds that are largely ineffective at crossing the blood-brain barrier (BBB) to exert an effect within central nervous system (CNS). The current study investigates the capacity of a novel small molecule modulator, initially believed to be a selective beta1-adrenergic receptor (ADRB1) partial agonist with high BBB permeability, to alleviate neuropathological changes in Thy1-hAPPLond/Swe+ (APP) and 5XFAD mouse models of AD. Tissue from two chronic dosing studies performed in these transgenic lines with the compound SHSU01010 was processed for evidence of three key neuropathological AD hallmarks – amyloid beta plaque accumulation, neuroinflammation, and synaptic loss. Data from immunohistochemistry, quantitative RT-PCR, and ELISA show that chronic SHSU01010 administration was ineffective at alleviating the AD neuropathology for which a phenotype was observed in APP and 5XFAD mice. We believe the lack of therapeutic efficacy exhibited by SHSU01010 may be attributed to its existence as two stereoisomers, where one may agonize beta-adrenergic signaling while the other antagonizes the system. Additionally, further characterization of SHSU01010 has demonstrated that the compound shows in vivo instability due to degradation of the compound by liver enzymes, and that the compound may not be selective for ADRB1. While the findings from the current study are inconclusive, we believe ADRB1 agonism with CNS-permeable compounds still shows substantial promise for AD treatment.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
